  "factCheck": {
    "status": "verified",
    "by": "HeyMariner daily desk",
    "checkedAt": "2026-11-17",
    "notes": "AMSA: HRU operates at depths between 1.5 and 4 m; for liferafts it cuts the cord holding the securing straps, the canister floats free, pulls out the painter which inflates the raft; the painter is attached to a weak link that breaks once the raft is inflated; common error: painter not attached to the weak link; disposable HRUs replaced at expiry, most about 2 years after installation; reusable units serviced annually. Hammar H20 manual: release depth 1.5-4.0 m per IMO/SOLAS; standard red weak link 2.2 +/- 0.4 kN; valid 2 years from month of installation. Painter-fast-to-ship consequence (raft held down) follows from the weak link function. Service intervals depend on unit type and maker."
  },
  "meta": {
    "description": "How a liferaft hydrostatic release unit works: water pressure fires a knife between 1.5 and 4 metres, the canister floats free, the painter inflates the raft and the weak link breaks so the raft is not dragged down. What to check on every LSA round and the rigging mistake that defeats the whole system.",
